# BATMAN live monitor — sample bitcoin.conf for a PRUNED full node on the D: drive.
#
# A pruned node connects to the real Bitcoin network and relays every new
# transaction (so the ZMQ feed is complete and live), but keeps only a small
# rolling window of block history — about 20 GB here, not the ~600 GB a full
# archival node needs. That is all the live monitor requires.
#
# 1. Install Bitcoin Core: https://bitcoincore.org/en/download/
# 2. Save this file as bitcoin.conf inside the datadir below (create the folder).
# 3. Start the node:  bitcoind -datadir=D:\BitcoinData -conf=D:\BitcoinData\bitcoin.conf
#    The first sync ("initial block download") downloads and verifies the chain
#    and takes several hours to a few days depending on your connection; it only
#    KEEPS the pruned ~20 GB. Leave it running.
# 4. When synced, run:  py -m src.core.backend.live.monitor --source zmq
#    or open the app and use the Live Monitor page (it uses the API by default;
#    set BATMAN_FEED=zmq or run the CLI with --source zmq for the node feed).

# --- Store everything on D: so the C: drive is untouched ---
datadir=D:\BitcoinData

# --- Pruned node: keep ~20 GB of recent blocks (minimum is 550 MB) ---
prune=20000

# --- Local JSON-RPC (the monitor reads transaction detail from here) ---
server=1
rpcbind=127.0.0.1
rpcallowip=127.0.0.1
# Authentication: the node writes a .cookie file in datadir that BATMAN reads
# automatically. To use a fixed password instead, uncomment and set these, then
# set BATMAN_RPC_USER / BATMAN_RPC_PASSWORD in your environment:
# rpcuser=batman
# rpcpassword=change-this-to-a-long-random-string

# --- ZMQ: publish every new transaction's id on this socket ---
# This is the live feed BATMAN subscribes to. Port must match BATMAN_ZMQ_ENDPOINT
# (default tcp://127.0.0.1:28333).
zmqpubhashtx=tcp://127.0.0.1:28333
# (optional, not required by BATMAN but handy)
zmqpubhashblock=tcp://127.0.0.1:28333
